[{:db/id 17592186171931 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-42c69049-36e8-578d-9dfb-2ff8bd5f3b90" "question-5b2461ac-ea52-57e7-a443-c8398ff2d539" "question-17f6b277-cb5c-5c63-9196-ebb57f21de7c" "question-098b8756-dace-5cc8-a065-55b217575530"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171737 :question/problem "Suppose $n=10-30p,$ where $p \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ Construct an argument that explains why $n$ is even." :question/worked-solution "An integer is *even* if it can be written as $2a,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is even since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =10-30p \\\\  & =2({{5-15p}_{⏟}}_{a}) \\\\  & =2a,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have defined $a=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5-15p},$ which is an integer." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171738 :question/problem "Suppose $n=3-4m,$ where $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ Construct an argument that explains why $n$ is odd." :question/worked-solution "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a + 1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is *odd* since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =3-4m \\\\  & =(-4m+2)+1 \\\\  & =2({{-2m+1}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have defined $a=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2m+1},$ which is an integer." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171735 :question/problem "Suppose $n=4m^2+2m-1,$ where $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ Construct an argument that explains why $n$ is odd." :question/worked-solution "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a + 1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNow, note the following regarding the expression $4m^2+2m-1{:}$\n\n- All terms, apart from the constant term, have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The constant term can be written as $-1 = -2+1.$\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{odd}}$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =4{m}^{2}+2m-1 \\\\  & =(4{m}^{2}+2m-2)+1 \\\\  & =2({{2{m}^{2}+m-1}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2m^2+m-1}$ is an integer." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171736 :question/problem "Suppose $n=2pq - 4q + 5,$ where $p,q \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ Construct an argument that explains why $n$ is odd." :question/worked-solution "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a + 1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNow, note the following regarding the expression $2pq - 4q + 5 {:}$\n\n- All terms, apart from the constant term, have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The constant term can be written as $5 = 4+1.$\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{odd}}$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =2pq-4q+5 \\\\  & =(2pq-4q+4)+1 \\\\  & =2({{pq-2q+2}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} pq - 2q + 2\\in \\mathbb Z }.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-42c69049-36e8-578d-9dfb-2ff8bd5f3b90" :question/id #uuid "42c69049-36e8-578d-9dfb-2ff8bd5f3b90" :question/math-academy-id "q-184106" :question/problem "Suppose $n=6p-14,$ where $p\\in Z.$ Complete the following argument showing that $n$ is even." :question/worked-solution "An integer is *even* if it can be written as $2a,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is even since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =6p-14 \\\\  & =2({{3p-7}_{⏟}}_{a}) \\\\  & =2a,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have defined $a=3p-7,$ which is an integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-140484ee-1073-56ec-8cf7-c80d2cf1a293" "answer-field-b17d5873-4c03-5914-a72e-80b638999a48" "answer-field-e2a024ba-a149-58ee-9ce5-47961212f948"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-140484ee-1073-56ec-8cf7-c80d2cf1a293" :answer-fiel