id "answer-field-a69764db-d288-5dc3-80d9-607e35ea718a" :answer-field/id #uuid "a69764db-d288-5dc3-80d9-607e35ea718a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-026880e2-e551-5990-8bbd-db4f549c4604" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ccbd974a-734f-5964-8642-55bcbff0da17" "answer-026880e2-e551-5990-8bbd-db4f549c4604" "answer-2abdb860-a6e4-51e1-a44e-f2025e141470"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccbd974a-734f-5964-8642-55bcbff0da17" :answer/id #uuid "ccbd974a-734f-5964-8642-55bcbff0da17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12+15p" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-026880e2-e551-5990-8bbd-db4f549c4604" :answer/id #uuid "026880e2-e551-5990-8bbd-db4f549c4604" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12-15p" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2abdb860-a6e4-51e1-a44e-f2025e141470" :answer/id #uuid "2abdb860-a6e4-51e1-a44e-f2025e141470" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15-12p" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-098b8756-dace-5cc8-a065-55b217575530" :question/id #uuid "098b8756-dace-5cc8-a065-55b217575530" :question/math-academy-id "q-184100" :question/problem "Suppose $n=8r+12,$ where $r\\in Z.$ Complete the following argument showing that $n$ is even." :question/worked-solution "An integer is *even* if it can be written as $2a,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is even since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =8r+12 \\\\  & =2({{4r+6}_{⏟}}_{a}) \\\\  & =2a,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have defined $a=4r+6,$ which is an integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-03551827-e123-5b11-994e-e604d0848229" "answer-field-933179b2-9397-54d3-8f68-1542e3d036e9" "answer-field-e0e32467-d258-5792-912f-2d011401b919"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-03551827-e123-5b11-994e-e604d0848229" :answer-field/id #uuid "03551827-e123-5b11-994e-e604d0848229" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-8b0ae3d3-461e-518b-a2ef-6ebb72f47c66" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8b0ae3d3-461e-518b-a2ef-6ebb72f47c66" "answer-86b4e305-71a0-5b2c-a156-caa0bfa676d6" "answer-4fe62597-9426-5c36-8531-92b9216aa537"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b0ae3d3-461e-518b-a2ef-6ebb72f47c66" :answer/id #uuid "8b0ae3d3-461e-518b-a2ef-6ebb72f47c66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4r+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86b4e305-71a0-5b2c-a156-caa0bfa676d6" :answer/id #uuid "86b4e305-71a0-5b2c-a156-caa0bfa676d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4r-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4fe62597-9426-5c36-8531-92b9216aa537" :answer/id #uuid "4fe62597-9426-5c36-8531-92b9216aa537" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6r+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-933179b2-9397-54d3-8f68-1542e3d036e9" :answer-field/id #uuid "933179b2-9397-54d3-8f68-1542e3d036e9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-fe919718-dfb7-5b86-b58a-532fa64bedc7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fe919718-dfb7-5b86-b58a-532fa64bedc7" "answer-4d003bb6-827a-53a4-8d54-ba68b4e87ec5" "answer-82de9101-1f0e-5fd2-b1cd-1f2bf4a89240"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe919718-dfb7-5b86-b58a-532fa64bedc7" :answer/id #uuid "fe919718-dfb7-5b86-b58a-532fa64bedc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2a" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d003bb6-827a-53a4-8d54-ba68b4e87ec5" :answer/id #uuid "4d003bb6-827a-53a4-8d54-ba68b4e87ec5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2a+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82de9101-1f0e-5fd2-b1cd-1f2bf4a89240" :answer/id #uuid "82de9101-1f0e-5fd2-b1cd-1f2bf4a89240" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2a-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e0e32467-d258-5792-912f-2d011401b919" :answer-field/id #uuid "e0e32467-d258-5792-912f-2d011401b919" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-19e1edd7-ac07-5fd1-8741-ac0f7bbf1706" :answer-field/choices ["answer-19e1edd7-ac07-5fd1-8741-ac0f7bbf1706" "answer-2cb73b38-d878-58db-97b3-1e21b1eef55e" "answer-c7e8e2da-0714-5069-a584-8955dc8c1848"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19e1edd7-ac07-5fd1-8741-ac0f7bbf1706" :answer/id #uuid "19e1edd7-ac07-5fd1-8741-ac0f7bbf1706" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4r+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2cb73b38-d878-58db-97b3-1e21b1eef55e" :answer/id #uuid "2cb73b38-d878-58db-97b3-1e21b1eef55e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4r-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7e8e2da-0714-5069-a584-8955dc8c1848" :answer/id #uuid "c7e8e2da-0714-5069-a584-8955dc8c1848" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6r+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173279 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to prove universal statements that involve the evenness and oddness of simple expressions. \n\nFirst, we have the following definitions:\n\n- Two integers have the **same parity** if they're *both* even or *both* odd.\n\n- Two integers have **opposite parity** if *one* of them is even and the *other* is odd.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The integers $2$ and $4$ have the *same* parity because they're both even.\n\n- The integers $1$ and $7$ have the *same* parity because they're both odd.\n\n- On the other hand, the integers $1$ and $6$ have *opposite* parity because $1$ is odd and $6$ is even." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173293 :tutorial/content "The list below gives the first few even integers:\n\n$$\n0, \\quad \\pm 2, \\quad \\pm 4, \\quad \\pm 6, \\quad \\pm 8, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nAn integer is even if it is a multiple of $2.$ And, since every integer is a multiple of its factors, every even integer can be written as \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}\\times {\\color{blue}{\\textrm{some other integer}}}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =2\\times 0 \\\\ 2 & =2\\times 1 \\\\ 4 & =2\\times 2 \\\\ 12 & =2\\times 6 \\\\ -24 & =2\\times (-12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSuppose we know that an integer $n$ is even. Then, we can rewrite $n$ as follows:\n\n$$\nn={\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer. \n\nThis choice of letter for the second factor is arbitrary: we chose ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ here, but we can use any letter we like! However, the following variables are commonly used to denote integers:\n\n$$\na, \\quad b, \\quad c, \\quad k, \\quad l, \\quad m, \\quad n, \\quad p, \\quad q, \\quad r, \\quad x, \\quad y, \\quad z\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173292 :tutorial/content "Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ $P:\\: \\forall n\\in\\mathbb Z,\\, 8n-4$ *is even.*\n\nTo prove this statement, we need to show that $8n-4$ can be written as $2{\\color{blue}{a}},$ where ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer. \n\nThe first step is to factor out $2$ from the expression.\n\n$$\n8n-4 = 2(4n-2)\n$$\n\nNext, we introduce a new variable ${\\color{blue}{a}},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{a}} = 4n-2.\n$$\n\nNotice that since $n\\in\\mathbb Z,$ we have that $4n-2\\in\\mathbb Z.$ In other words, ${\\color{blue}{a}}\\in\\mathbb Z.$ It's essential to state this since the whole argument breaks down if the second factor is sometimes not an integer. \n\nNow, our expression can be written as\n\n$$\n2(4n-2) = 2{\\color{blue}{a}}.\n$$\n\nSo, since $8n-4$ can be written in the form $2{\\color{blue}{a}}$ where ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer, we conclude that $8n-4$ is even *for all* $n\\in\\mathbb Z.$\n\nLet's take a look at another example. Before we do, note that statements about parity are often written informally, and so the universal quantifier $\\forall$ is usually hidden." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173280 :tutorial/content "Recall that an integer is *odd* if it is not even. The list below gives the first few odd integers:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1, \\quad \\pm 3, \\quad \\pm 5, \\quad \\pm 7, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nNotice that every odd integer can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}\\times {\\color{blue}{\\textrm{some other integer}}} + 1.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =2\\times 0+1 \\\\ 3 & =2\\times 1+1 \\\\ 5 & =2\\times 2+1 \\\\ 19 & =2\\times 9+1 \\\\ -17 & =2\\times (-9)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, an integer $n$ is odd if it can be written as \n\n$$\nn={\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}+1\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}a$ is an integer." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173294 :tutorial/content "Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ $P:\\: \\forall n\\in\\mathbb Z,\\, 4n+7$ *is odd.*\n\nTo prove this statement, we need to show that it can be written as $2{\\color{blue}{a}}+1,$ where ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer. \n\nThe first step is to rewrite the expression by isolating $+1.$ We can do this by decomposing $7$ into $6+1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4n+7 = 4n+6+1 = (4n+6)+1\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out $2$ from the expression in parentheses:\n\n$$\n(4n+6)+1 = 2(2n+3) + 1\n$$\n\nThen, we introduce a new variable ${\\color{blue}{a}},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{a}} = 2n+3.\n$$\n\nNotice that since $n\\in\\mathbb Z,$ we have that $2n+3\\in\\mathbb Z.$ In other words, ${\\color{blue}{a}}\\in\\mathbb Z.$ \n\nThis means our original expression can be written as\n\n$$\n2(2n+3) + 1= 2{\\color{blue}{a}} + 1.\n$$\n\nSo, since $4n+7$ can be written in the form $2{\\color{blue}{a}}+1$ where ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer, we conclude that $4n+7$ is odd *for all* $n\\in\\mathbb Z.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]}]
